WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.320
- [Narrator] In her first two years

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.760
at the Federal Trade Commission,

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.830
Chair Lina Khan has already taken on

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.840
the biggest tech titans in the world.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.690
- Her beef is with the biggest company.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.670
- [Narrator] And she's
lost big to some of them.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.320
- I'm certainly not somebody who thinks

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.890
that success is marked by a
hundred percent court record.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.020
- [Narrator] That strategy
has made her a lightning rod

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.090
for controversy and criticism.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.770
- This raises the question, Chair Khan,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.210
are you losing on purpose?

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.770
- [Narrator] So what drives her to take on

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.550
the toughest fights, and what
happens if she doesn't win?

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.420
- Antitrust lawyers say
that this is as aggressive

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.360
as they've seen the FTC
in a very long time.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.880
- Over the last century,
we've seen time and time again

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.730
that when we have robust
antitrust enforcement

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:45.000
and robust competition,
that really sets us up best

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.490
to have the strongest,
most thriving economy.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.640
- [Narrator] In 2021, the year
Khan was sworn in as Chair,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.810
the FTC issued 42 letters
of investigation over M&amp;A,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.120
that's almost twice the amount in 2020,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.220
and the highest in more than a decade.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.560
- When the FTC does investigations,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.920
they type their own
resources, and they tie a deal

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.830
in knots for as long as a year.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.500
- [Narrator] And investigating
more deals can slow down

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.270
the merger market overall.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.700
- More important is the chilling effect

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.240
that they're having on
M&amp;A activity in general.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.350
- [Narrator] Stoller is a friend

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.090
and former coworker of Khan.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.160
- They're trying to address activities

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.550
that are central to coercive behavior

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:31.550
in the economy right now by
challenging vertical mergers

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.070
by big companies, dominant
infrastructure players.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.300
- [Narrator] And it's not just the number

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.460
of mergers the FTC investigates.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.430
It's also the type of company
the agency is scrutinizing.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:45.720
- We are also entirely
outgunned, especially when we are

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.420
up against some of the most
formidable and wealthiest

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.610
and most powerful companies in America.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.080
- [Narrator] But that hasn't stopped Khan

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.660
from taking on companies
like Meta or Microsoft.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.580
- Lina's supporters do think

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.460
that she's bringing the
cases that the government

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.560
was too chicken in the past to bring.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.850
- [Narrator] But those big shots

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.890
haven't necessarily panned out.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:08.100
In February, the FTC lost
its case to block Meta

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.710
from acquiring virtual
reality company Within.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.920
And in early July, a federal
judge sided with Microsoft

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.260
after the agency sued to block its merger

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:17.910
with gaming company, Activision.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.130
The FTC appealed and the court rejected

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.430
the agency's request to block
the deal in the meantime.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.930
In a statement, Meta said

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.300
that the deal would bring
pro-competitive benefits

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.440
to the ecosystem.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.180
Microsoft declined to comment.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.640
Despite the losses, Khan
has said it's important

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.290
to take those cases to court.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.690
- As public enforcers, we
have a special obligation

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.480
to be bringing the hard cases
because if you don't try,

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.460
the message that sends
out to the world is that

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.680
the enforcers don't think
there's a problem in the market.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.530
- [Narrator] Still, there's
a risk in losing too much.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.260
- It creates bad precedent for you,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.000
and perhaps even Wall Street

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.040
and the companies start to tune you out

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.380
and think it's worth fighting
you 'cause you're not winning.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.420
Lawyers I've spoken to, they say,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.550
maybe the FTC should take a step back

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.580
and think more deeply
about the kinds of deals

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.160
that they wanna bring
that will make the points

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.840
that Lina's trying to make

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.520
- [Narrator] In a
statement, the agency said

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.050
that in the last year and a half,

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.590
the FTC has had 20 wins
including several mergers

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.020
that were abandoned
following FTC scrutiny.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.150
The cases that Khan's FTC has brought

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.860
are not always straightforward.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.770
Microsoft and Activision
aren't direct competitors

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.560
so the agency needed the court
to buy into its prediction

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.290
that the merger could hurt
competition in the future.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.110
The courts have had mixed
responses to that legal theory.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.260
- Lina says that over
the last 30 odd years

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.320
that the law came to put too much focus

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.510
on what's called the
consumer welfare standard,

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:44.370
which is measuring harm from
deals or from practices,

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.570
measuring that in consumer
prices or supply output.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.050
Lina disavows this
consumer welfare standard

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:55.680
is really the purpose
of the antitrust laws.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.740
- What Lina Khan is doing
is to put competition

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.530
at the center of antitrust policy again.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.760
And to say, actually it's about this law

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.320
is about whether you can let people

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.480
into the market or not to compete.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.520
- [Narrator] This approach
is part of what got Khan

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.630
to the FTC.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.550
The youngest FTC chair in history,

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.200
she'd already made a name for herself

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.730
by the time she was appointed.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:21.000
After graduating from college,
khan worked at a think tech

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.160
where she looked at
corporate consolidation.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.870
- It became clear pretty quickly

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.390
that there had been a pretty steady trend

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.940
of consolidation across the board.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.767
- [Narrator] And in 2017, she
published an article titled

00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:34.740
"Amazon's Antitrust Paradox"

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.390
when she was still in law school.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:41.190
- That paper reimagines the
thrust of the antitrust laws.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:44.160
She wants to think about
antitrust enforcement

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.270
and competitive harm
in a much broader way.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.340
- She essentially said,
the way that we do business

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.180
in America is, essentially,
should be illegal.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.680
- Amazon declined to comment.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.350
That paper made her an
antitrust celebrity.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.500
- She's a symbol of a new approach

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.840
to addressing monopoly power.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.210
- She has come to prominence
at a certain moment

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.480
when technology plays a
huge, huge role in our lives,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.460
and that's not gonna change in the future,

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.900
it's only gonna get bigger.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.820
- [Narrator] When President
Biden came into office,

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.460
he made antitrust a
big part of his agenda.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.530
- I'm a capitalist, but capitalism

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.278
without competition is not capitalism.

00:05:22.278 --> 00:05:23.430
(audience applaud)

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.370
Capitalism without
competition is exploitation.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:30.360
- When he made her Chair, it
was an indication of policy.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.710
And that was a surprise to me

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.330
and I think it was a
surprise to a lot of people.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.430
President Biden thinks
we have a monopoly crisis

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.270
and that our monopoly crisis is a serious

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.520
and core political economy issue

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.850
that he wants to address
as part of his presidency.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.680
- [Narrator] But Khan's
appointment was met with calls

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.910
for her to recuse herself

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:51.090
from cases involving companies
she'd criticized in the past.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:55.260
- Facebook is seeking the
recusal of FTC Chair Lina Kahn.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.470
- I have to tell you that
Amazon's right when they say

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:02.010
that they're entitled to
impartial commissioners.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.390
- It's not a surprise that companies

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.190
are gonna throw everything that they have

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:06.870
at the wall to see what sticks.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.040
- [Narrator] She didn't recuse herself

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.210
from the Meta/Within case

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.580
despite an ethics
officer's recommendation,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:12.810
prompting blowback.

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:15.870
- There was no violation
under the ethics laws

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.870
because I have not a
penny in financial stock.

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:20.370
- [Narrator] Khan is under investigation

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.320
by three Republican-led house committees

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.270
for her leadership at the FTC.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.070
The agency declined to comment,

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.980
but criticism has come
from within the agency too.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.320
Republican Commissioner
Christine Wilson resigned

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.980
in early 2023, accusing
Khan of an abuse of power.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.110
The Commission, which
is usually bipartisan,

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.180
is now solely democratic.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:40.650
In a February statement,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.600
Khan and the other commissioners said

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.910
that while they often
disagreed with Wilson,

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.220
they respect her devotion to her beliefs.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.890
Khan has also faced employee discontent.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:53.160
- Lina Kahn came in
from the start and said

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.770
that the FTC had done
a lot of things wrong.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.410
While that was appealing
to her supporters,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:02.220
it was probably alienating
to some of her staff

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:07.170
and perhaps also to other
former commissioners

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:08.610
or other former chairs.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:12.000
- We are fortunate to have
consummate professionals,

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.820
extremely talented staff at the FTC.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.470
Of course, we work hard to make sure

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.620
that the FTC remains
a great place to work.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.720
- [Narrator] But despite
criticism and skepticism

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:25.230
around her strategy, Khan
says she isn't going anywhere.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:28.320
- My term goes until, through 2024,

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:30.270
and I fully intend to continue serving.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:32.370
- [Narrator] Including drafting
aggressive new guidelines

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:33.720
with the justice department

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.720
on how the agency will
investigate new mergers.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.150
- It doesn't seem like the FTC has a lot

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.160
of time to change its strategy.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.820
So Lina Kahn has a relatively short window

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.888
to accomplish as much as she can.

00:07:46.888 --> 00:07:49.471
(bright music)

